            <ArticleTitle>[Primary infection with cytomegalovirus: An infectious cause of splenic infarction].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>555-557</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0248-8663(16)31056-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.revmed.2016.12.017</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cytomegalovirus-associated thrombosis has been extensively reported in the medical literature, mainly in immune-compromised patients. However, the association with splenic infarcts has rarely been reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a 32-year-old Caucasian and immunocompetent woman who presented with a splenic infarction during a primary infection with CMV. The differential diagnostic ruled out embolic, hematologic, gastrointestinal and coagulation disorders. The outcome was favorable with symptomatic treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A primary infection with CMV must be added to the diagnostic work-up in the presence of a febrile splenic infarction, especially when it is associated with a biological mononucleosis reaction.</AbstractText>
